<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>The UK unemployment rate has falle=
n</strong>, in a pre-budget boost to chancellor John Healey, but the number=
 of vacancies continue to shrink.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The latest data on the UK labour market, j=
ust released, shows that the UK unemployment rate fell to 4.7% in June to A=
ugust, down from 4.9% a month ago. That=E2=80=99s better than expected =E2=
=80=93 City economists had forecast a rise to 5%.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In its final snapshot of Britain=E2=80=99s=
 jobs market before the budget in two week=E2=80=99s time, the Office for N=
ational Statistics reports that employment rose, by 102,000 jobs, to 34.577=
m in the last quarter. The number of unemployed fell by 47,000 to 1.712m, a=
nd there was a small (16,000) drop in people =E2=80=98economically inactive=
=E2=80=99, to 9.091m.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Worryingly, however,</strong> the =
number of vacancies has seen its largest fall since the start of the year, =
falling by 21,000 in the last quarter. That pulled the number of vacancies =
down to 691,000 in July to September, down from more than 1.2m in early 202=
2. This is the first time the total has been below 700,000 since February t=
o April 2021.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The ONS says: =E2=80=9CThis is mainly from=
 smaller firms, who continue to suggest that they may not be recruiting bec=
ause of increased labour costs.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">That=E2=80=99s a reference to the higher e=
mployer national insurance contributions brought in by Healey=E2=80=99s pre=
decessor, Rachel Reeves, and the minimum wage rise, which have made hiring =
more costly. Vacancies fell particularly sharply across the construction se=
ctor, suggesting Britain=E2=80=99s builders cut back on hiring last summer.=
</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;"><strong>Regular pay growth rose in the las=
t quarter</strong>,but once you account for bonuses, wage growth slowed. Pa=
y continued to rise faster in the public sector than in the private sector.=
 Regular annual growth was 3.0% for the private sector and 6.3% for the pub=
lic sector.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">However, as the ONS points out, public sec=
tor pay growth continues to be affected by variations in the timings of pay=
 awards this year =E2=80=93 largely down to pay deals in the NHS.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Ben Harrison, director of the Work Foundat=
ion at Lancaster University, says the government should not be complacent a=
bout the labour market: =E2=80=9CThe UK is still amidst a jobs drought with=
 vacancies at their lowest for over 12 years outside of the pandemic.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CIn particular, there are now 715,=
000 young people aged 16=E2=80=9324 unemployed, down by 28,000 on the quart=
er. Nevertheless, the number of young people out of work remains 54,000 hig=
her than 12 months ago.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CFor too many young people there a=
re simply not enough suitable jobs available to them. Overall, vacancies ha=
ve fallen to 691,000 =E2=80=93 the lowest level outside the pandemic since =
2014 =E2=80=93 while Work Foundation research shows that adverts for starte=
r jobs have almost halved over the past.</p>
